<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Deliberately almost empty, and that is the claim worth making.

	     Screen Recording is not an entitlement. It is a TCC permission the user grants at
	     runtime and can revoke in System Settings, which is the only privileged thing Arin
	     ever holds. Nothing here can grant it.

	     Not present, and each absence is load bearing:

	       - No App Sandbox. A cask-installed app is not sandboxed, and claiming the
	         sandbox while reading a Unix socket in the user's temporary directory would
	         mean asking for exceptions that add up to more than not claiming it.
	       - No Accessibility, and no way to ask for it. Accessibility is what would let
	         Arin click and type, and never needing it is the product boundary CI enforces.
	       - No `com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` or
	         `disable-library-validation`. The hardened runtime is on with no holes in it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
